  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166813,
      "id": "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b",
      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9f5904bb-8a33-54b2-abd7-bb94bf242ef5",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are eighth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{5}+{x}^{6}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{4}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{3}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of eighth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c7cf67da-c4da-5f76-80e3-b7115a294620",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
