    "question": {
      ":question/id": "82b0bb99-d8f3-56d6-ae60-dd85d742453d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are eighth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{3}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{4}+{x}^{7}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of eighth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
