    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f10b6688-92b7-584b-b6ec-926e41ddf008",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are fourth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{3}+{x}^{10}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{4}={x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{2}={x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fourth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a fourth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a fourth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a fourth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
