    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "80e8e6f4-196a-5d70-9f1c-cd089052bed6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are eighth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{6}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{‴}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{3}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{3}+{x}^{6}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of eighth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "69eb896f-9318-568e-b96e-5ca45b699469",
