      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fifth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
