      ":question/id": "fe0e663c-28e1-5afe-aac0-0249d4698673",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are fifth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{3}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{5}+{x}^{10}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{3}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fifth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
