      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are seventh-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{2}+{x}^{10}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{4}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of seventh-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is also a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
