  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166813,
      "id": "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b",
      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "eafa80d5-4873-5321-b23d-06a86142feaf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are eighth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{2}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}})^{2}={x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{2}+{x}^{10}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of eighth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "41724306-fb79-59b9-9ca6-6c0635d8d40b",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
