      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are sixth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{3}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **II.** ${x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{‴}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}})^{5}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{4}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of sixth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a sixth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a sixth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a sixth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
