      "id": "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b",
      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3c4016f8-0fb6-5a2b-9fa8-d2edfbcd13c5",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are fifth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{‴}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{3}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{5}+{x}^{7}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{4}={x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fifth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
